{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Hear O Israel, YHWH our Elohim, YHWH is one.</b> Without three mentions it is impossible, for if he said 'Hear O Israel, YHWH is one,' every single nation would say concerning their idol that it is one. When he says 'our Elohim,' it is known that he is speaking of the Elohim of Israel. And if we were to say 'YHWH is one,' it would imply 'our Elohim is one of the elohim.' All the more so if we were to say 'our Elohim is one.' But when we double the letter heh, this is what it says: YHWH, who is our master — He is one in mastery, and there is none besides Him. And one must say 'El, faithful King' before 'Hear O Israel,' because in these three words together with what follows them, you will find in the Qeri'at Shema 248 words, corresponding to 248 limbs. And anyone who says the 248 words in the Qeri'at Shema according to their proper order — the Holy One, blessed be He, guards his 248 limbs. As it is said: ״שמור מצותי וחיה״ — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: Guard what is Mine and I will guard what is yours. Rabbi Shimon ben Chalafta said: A parable — to a king who had a vineyard in Judea and his dwelling in the Galilee, and to another man there was a vineyard in the Galilee and his dwelling in Judea. Every single year this one in Judea needs to go to the Galilee, and this one in the Galilee needs to go to Judea, each to provide for the needs of his vineyard. One day they met one another. They said: Why are we troubling ourselves? I will provide for your vineyard that is in my city, and you will provide for my vineyard that is in your city. And be careful with it — if you guard what is mine well, I will guard what is yours well.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "The phrase 'וכשאנו כופלים ה״ק ה״ש' (when we double the letter heh) refers to the letter heh appearing twice in the divine name YHWH; 'ה״ק' is read as 'ה״ש' per the context. The parable's closing words preserve a textual difficulty: the switch to second-person address ('if you guard... I will guard') may reflect unmarked dialogue between the two men in the parable."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:38.191523628Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}